n capacity of time  varying channels and my thesis or the  the committee was Jean and Praveen and  Jim Pitman it was one of my favorite  professors of probability and so I  launched into my talk and first couple  slides go fine and then I'm getting a  little confident kind of getting on a  roll and then comes slide five I went  back and let slide 5 so here I'm talking  about the continuous time channel  capacity where you kind of decompose the  channel you need carpet and web  decomposition and and then John asks  what does this have to do with hilbert  spaces okay well the correct answer at  that time for me was I don't know what a  hilbert spaces I remember I took real  analysis as an undergraduate and so here  David Hilbert raises his head in my exam  and of course I have no idea how to  answer this question and so my vision of  getting a PhD and going on and becoming  a professor is vanishing at a very rapid  rate and it didn't come to me a Hilbert  space just for those of you that don't  remember it's a special case of a vector  space of house enorm and it's complete  so all limits limits of limits are in  the space and all that but I didn't  remember any of this now if at the time  I was it skilled at deflecting questions  that I don't have the answer to I would  have said nothing can we move on right  because that's a reasonable  any question especially if you don't  know the answer but of course I was not  skilled in deflecting questions or being  an arrogant academic and saying  something like that in a thesis defense  so I kind of stuttered and and got a  little choked up thinking that my degree  was going out the window and all this  somehow I did manage to get through the  exam and I passed so then I was moving  on to the thesis writing and and Shaun  signed off on my thesis - so did Praveen  and so there I was as a graduate you  know got my PhD and what I realized at  that point is that that made me Shaun's  academic sibling because he got his PhD  from