also you've  been a wonderful friend all these years  so anyway with that I'm gonna launch  into talking about this wireless  connection and what's next for for  wireless networks note that John's talk  was about 4G networks what we're moving  on to 5g so I'm gonna talk about what's  new and exciting in in the next  generation of wireless networks so as  you've seen I've been working in  Wireless my whole career 30 years and to  me it's a really exciting time to be in  Wireless maybe the most exciting time  because in the past working in Wireless  was really about either people talking  to other people over wireless  connections or accessing information  over the Internet  and now we have so many more ways that  we want to use Wireless whether it's in  sensor networks or in devices inside our  bodies all this automation of highways  of smart cities of the energy grid and  so there's a lot of interesting new  applications which require us to build  different types of networks and we've  had in the past one of the big  challenges is the fact that there's very  limited spectrum so this is a graph from  a couple years ago which said that as of  2013 the license spectrum which is what  the cellular phones use is 300 or is is  full and of course we've gone on since  20:13 to build more and more wireless  devices and use more and more spectrum  but we're not using it in the licensed  band we're using it over Wi-Fi on the  horizon is millimeter-wave where there's  tens of gigahertz of spectrum and one of  the new things that I'm working on is  molecular communication so why not try  to use molecules to send information so  I'm going to talk about that a little  bit at the end so if the license bans  are full now how are we going to support  the internet of things so the notion of  the Internet of Things is that every  single device with an on-off switch is  going to be able to connect to each  other and connect to the backbone  Internet and that's a very different  type of network than we've built up  until now in particular because these  devices so maybe in my tennis shoes or  in my sensor in my backyard or in my  thermostat these are devices that have  very low energy sources and they may  actually not even have a battery and  also low data rates so up until now  we've been pushing for higher and higher  data rates and now we're going to  devices that maybe need bits per second  the interesting thing to me about the  Internet of Things is that it's not so  much that it's important that my tennis  shoes talk to John's tennis shoes or I  guess not tennis shoes I what's  important is that collecting all this  data and sending it up into the cloud is  where and doing the processing in the  cloud and figuring out how to utilize  all this data that's where the value of  the Internet of Things is and so that  means we need a communication  infrastructure that can do all this for  devices that again have very limited  energy and maybe need to last the  lifetime of a building or of a car or of  a bridge and so forth so so how are we  going to build networks like this one of  the questions that I asked because I  still work in Shannon capacity is are we  at the Shannon capacity limit of  wireless systems so I'll just take a  show of hands how many people think  we're at the Shannon capacity limit of  wireless systems raise your hand how  many people think we're not  okay so you're all wrong because we  don't know the Shannon capacity of most  wireless channels and that actually goes  back to my PhD research we were looking  then at how to compute the Shannon  capacity of wireless channels and it's a  hard problem in most cases so if we  don't have a model which is the true  four millimeter wave channels we don't  have good models for that certainly not  for terahertz not for molecular channels  if we don't have a channel model you  can't compute unit capacity if the  channel is time varying even though I  got results for that in my thesis in  some special cases for most time varying  channels we don't know the capacity we  don't know the capacity with  interference or relays that's been an  open problem basically since the  beginning of Shannon theory cellular  systems have interference and so they  introduce interference so we don't know  capacity of those systems don't know  capacity of multi hoc networks and as  soon as we introduce things like delay  or energy or dollar constraints you  can't drive the probability of error to  zero which is the underlying premise of  Shannon capacity so we can't bring in  things like hardware constraints or  limited energy into solving sham Pasi so  I'd say well then why do we work in  chanock capacity if it doesn't tell us  anything interesting about designing  these systems in fact that's not true so  Shannon capacity does provide in my  experience a lot of interesting insights  into the design of wireless systems as  well as performance upper bounds so I'm  going to talk a little bit about some of  the enablers for this next generation of  networks including utilizing millimeter  wave spectrum along with massive MIMO  rethinking cellular system design  completely through a notion called  software-defined wireless networking a  little bit about new physical layer and  multiple axis techniques and then green  networks now you might say well why am I  talking about new physical layer design  physical layer it's been dead multiple  times so it was first declared dead in  1971 when they said coating was dead  then the 80s it was dead in 2014 it was  also dead but there actually is some  life in new physical layer techniques  which I'll talk about briefly okay  so what's up with millimeter wave  there's tens of gigahertz spectrum up  there so everything  commercial system today fits into the  millimeter wave bands which is why  people are starting to look at it the  problem is that when you go to these  very high frequencies path loss is  proportional to 1 over lambda squared so  you get very high path loss it's very  difficult to communicate at large  distances if you're just sending things  through an omnidirectional antenna so  what you do is you build these large  antenna arrays which can point a very  pencil-thin beam in any particular  receiver and therefore you don't have  that same kind of path loss and you can  get a strong signal at the receiver in  addition once you form these kind of  pencil-thin rays everything that we've  worked on for decades in terms of  enabling wireless communications goes  away so there's no interference because  that beam is not causing interference to  any of the other radios there's no  multipath fading because there's no  reflections that arrive at the receiver  delayed in time and phase and cause the  random changes in the received signal  strength and there's very little  attenuation because again you're  channeling all of your energy in exactly  the direction that you want to go so you  might say well great this solves all of  our problems we don't need to do any  more communication theory and the other  nice thing is you say well but that's a  very big array however when we go to  millimeter waves the antenna elements  are small so you can build these arrays  and they're not so big so I like to call  this millimeter wave mass of my mode the  dynamic duo 5g communication because  they kind of compensate for each other's  strengths and weaknesses and and so  there's a lot of excitement about  building these massive MIMO systems for  millimeter waves the problem is that in  order to form this very fine beam you  need to know the channel perfectly  between every transmit antenna and every  receive antenna and that's very hard to  do and in addition if you put an object  in between the antenna array and the  receiver this whole nice beautiful  pencil-thin beam goes away and it gets  scattered and so all of these benefits  go away so it isn't the panacea that we  would like for 5g communications but  it's certainly a piece of what's going  to be in this next  generation of systems okay so rethinking  secular system design here's another  question when was the paper that laid  out how cellular system designs are  architected or how cellular systems are  architected written which decade so  first cellular system was deployed in  the 1980s so it was before that so how  many people think it was the 1970s raise  your hand that's when Shawn came to  Berkeley okay how many people think it  was the 1960s 1950s 1940s it was the  forties it was a paper by D H ring from  eighteen T Bell Labs that laid out if  you read that paper basically lays out  exactly the architecture of cellular  systems you have a base station in the  middle of a cell the toxology uses in  the cell you do hand off this is all  written in the 1948 paper and we still  design systems the exact same way okay  of course we've advanced what we do at  the physical layer we're no longer using  analog we're using digital and all these  sophisticated techniques but the basic  architecture of cellular systems have  not changed even though we've  incorporated all these techniques so you  can think about new ways to design  cellular systems where you can have the  base stations cooperate that was  incorporated in the 3gpp standard you  can have relays you can have a mix of  small and large cells you can distribute  your antennas throughout a cell these  are all things that have been  incorporated into research and also some  experimental systems but we've never  gone back and said well maybe we should  rethink the whole architecture and if we  did that then we might be able to get  big gains either in capacity over what  we have now or perhaps in other metrics  like coverage I live off Sand Hill Road  most expensive real estate probably in  the world and every time I Drive up to  280 my cell phone call gets dropped I  mean this is a voice call this is not a  high capacity call so we still don't get  good coverage and that's going to be a  real problem if we're using these  networks to do autonomous driving  and then the energy constraints that the  internet-of-things introduced if we are  going to start worrying about supporting  devices that are running off small  batteries or collecting energy from the  Sun or from vibrations we need to focus  more on the energy consumption of the  network than on the data rates that  we're going to get so small cells is  something that certainly gives us a  boost in capacity and the reason is that  if you build an architecture so that  every cell can support say 100 users and  then you shrink the size of the cell and  you still support the same hundred users  overall your system capacity grows  exponentially so it's been known for  quite a long time that going to smaller  cells gives us higher capacity the  problem is how do you optimize all these  small cells and this gets into doing  optimization in the cloud to manage the  parameters and the resource allocation  of the different users and so this is a  notion called sawn self-organizing  networks where you just send the  parameters of the network up into the  cloud and you do some kind of  optimization and then send back the  parameters so if you do that for  cellular you could do that for all  Network so you could do that for Wi-Fi  you could do that for millimeter waves  vehicular networks and so forth and this  kind of gives rise to this notion of  software-defined wireless networks so  Nick McEwen who did you know the  OpenFlow we were graduate students  together in fact the very first  probability class I ever took as a  graduate student was with Nick so he's  coined the notion of software-defined  networks for Wired networks wireless  it's a little bit different because here  you've got a different set of resources  to allocate and the way you allocate the  resources impacts the different networks  so for example if Wi-Fi and cellular or  sharing spectrum as is the case now with  unlicensed LTE you want to manage that  spectrum between the two different  networks so if you talk about how do you  do frequence out allocation this unified  control plane can allocate frequencies  across the different networks it can  allocate different power it can decide  which network a particular application  should go over so if I have a video say  an HD video I want to go over a  millimeter  wavenet work because it has the highest  bandwidth but when I move away from that  Network I need to do a seamless handoff  to another network so this is the notion  of the software-defined network  architecture and in fact if you want to  go to the shangri-la of what this means  you say well I don't need any systems at  all I'm just going to have a bunch of  distributed antennas at different  frequencies and let the unified control  plane figure out exactly how to utilize  these antennas for the different systems  of course there's a lot of challenges in  doing this the algorithmic complexity is  one as someone who worked on frequency  allocation and second generation  networks that was a np-hard problem it's  a graph coloring problem and now things  are even harder because we have multiple  antennas and power control and small and  large cells and so forth so I call this  an NP really hard problem it's not just  np-hard but fortunately thanks to many  people here and and and colleagues  elsewhere there's a lot of advanced  optimization tools to solve these  optimization problems but one of the  things that's kind of unique to wireless  is that we don't need to look at this  just in terms of distributed  optimization or centralized optimization  so if you can't get all the information  to the cloud because of the latency or  the complexity of doing things in the  cloud you could do things fully  distribute it and that's how Wi-Fi works  generally your Wi-Fi access point is  doing optimization fully distributed but  there's this notion of fog optimization  where now you can kind of form these  small neighborhoods where you do  centralized optimization within a  neighborhood and you can scale the size  of the neighborhoods such that if it's  very small it's fully distributed  optimization if it's very large then  it's fully centralized optimization and  you can look at how the performance  changes with the scaling of the size of  the network and this is something that I  think is or a size of the neighborhood I  think it's something that's really  important for wireless network  optimization we have some work on it but  I'm not aware and maybe people are have  seen in another context of a real theory  behind looking at this fog optimization  and how much can be gained over  distributed and  how much is lost versus centralized okay  a couple other things I just want to  mention so in fact there are new  physical layer techniques in terms of  wave forms for 5g that are robust to  changing channels that are more  bandwidth and energy efficient and then  with the Internet of Things we need new  ways of doing multiple access because  chopping things up in an orthogonal  manner is not efficient when you have  these very small devices so there's kind  of a whole new set of access techniques  that we can take a look at and one of  the other things that we're doing in my  group now is machine learning now I'm  not people that know me I'm not a big  bandwagon person like if everybody's  working in machine learning I'm going to  run the other direction typically but it  turns out that machine learning is very  useful in certain aspects of  communications particularly when you  don't have a channel model so when does  machine learning work well it works well  when you don't in models in most  communication system designs we have  good models for most wireless networks  we have good models for the channel but  as we move to things like millimeter  wave and terahertz and molecular there  are no good models and it turns out that  we first applied this to a molecular  communication system we're working on  because there was no alternative when  you don't have a channel model and we  got a 10x gain in data rate by using  this for our detector to remove the  inter symbol interference so it's we've  been doing some other work on applying  machine learning and contacts where  either you don't have good channel  models or network models or you have  good models but you don't know the  parameters it's too expensive to learn  the parameters so I think that's an  interesting new marionette to look at  and so that brings me to the chemical  communication so the way I got into  chemical communication is I had a  postdoc a student the graduate student  from York University who had built a  chemical communication system there it's  an on/off keying system so you're  basically now embedding bits in  chemicals instead of electromagnetic  signals so the system that he built was  an on-off keying system with vodka okay  so one send vodka zero  don't send vodka and there's a  breathalyzer on the other end and the  problem with the brothel I  is that just like you know we're gonna  do tonight to celebrate John's  retirement you get saturated eventually  yeah I don't know if it'll be with vodka  but eventually the sensor gets saturated  and you can't communicate anymore  so so he came to my group and we started  working I thought this was a pretty cool  thing to look at because it's completely  Greenfield okay the the way that signals  propagate is chemicals it's through  diffusion so it's not Maxwell's  equations it's diffusion equations so  you don't have good channel models and  everything about designing the system is  new how do you modulate bits how do you  send them through the channel how do you  mitigate the impairments of the channel  how do you have multiple users sharing  this channel how do you do networking  this is all completely new as are the  fundamental capacity limits so it's a  very fun area to work on and we actually  have a system in our lab right now so  because the vodka was not very efficient  and only got about a half a bit per  second which you might say well that's  ridiculous but actually if you think  about using this foreign body  communication to turn on artificial  organs or to do drug delivery  adjustments half a bit per second isn't  bad we got up to an order of magnitude  above that using two chemicals so now we  use an acid and a base Windex and  vinegar basically and so Stanford report  did an article about sending text  messages using Windex and vinegar but of  course now saying the undergraduates  probably wouldn't vodkas more fun yes  but how do you cancel out the alcohol is  the problem Bloody Mary's don't work as  far as I know so anyway so so we applied  machine learning to equalize the tail of  the system response which again this is  through diffusion so it takes a long  time for the chemicals to diffuse and so  we're continuing to work on this it's a  lot of fun and the last thing I'll talk  about before wrapping up is some new  work that I started as part of the NSF  Center for science of information come  or as part of that as well where we  wanted to take our ideas of  communications and information theory  and apply them beyond communication  systems so I was thinking about well  what looks like network outside of  traditional communication system so I  thought well the brain looks like a  network I was naive  I thought it was a wireless network and  then it turns out that the way these  signals propagate through the brain are  through these neuronal chains which are  actually wired not Wireless and so we  looked at a bunch of different things to  try to understand where we could apply  our techniques to to neuroscience and  what we came up with in the end was  looking at epileptic seizures to try to  find the focal point of an elective  seizure so for patients with epilepsy  that's drug resistant the only way to  stop the seizures is to cut out the part  of the brain where the seizure starts so  you get one neuron that starts  oscillating very quickly that spreads to  surrounding neurons because they're all  connected and eventually the  oscillations spread enough so that you  have a seizure so if you can go back and  find that seizure onset zone you can  give the doctors better information  about where they should be cutting  because the way they do it now is the  doctors will implant this sensor they'll  take a look at this grid and say oh I  think this is where this this sensor is  where the the seizure onset sonja's so  we introduced an algorithm based on  directed mutual information that's  basically reverse engineering so it's  looking at these sensor data and reverse  engineering through causality back to  where the signal started where the the  seizure started the seizure on its own  and it turns out we've run this on about  20 different data sets and our algorithm  beats the doctors what I mean by that is  that the data sets they annotate where  the doctor identified this seizure once  its own our algorithm gives a smaller  zone and it matches the doctors in every  case now we haven't gone back and looked  at what we're trying to do now is say  well if our our algorithm did not match  the doctor and they did the operation  was it successful or was it not  successful because that would give us  more information about whether when we  don't agree with the doctors if if we're  more accurate or less accurate so let me  wrap up I 5g networks a lot of fun I  hope you're not going to stop working in  Wireless because we could use your  insight and ideas there's a non  going to higher data rates for better  cell phone usage but also for all these  Internet of Things devices I think the  notion of this cloud-based software to  manage Wireless and how how we can do  that optimally through you know this fog  optimization is very interesting at the  physical layer using small cells in  massive MIMO there's a lot of research  to be there done there as well and then  looking at energy efficient design so  let me just say last John can't believe  you're retiring it makes me feel really  old best wishes on your retirement thank  you for your incredible contributions to  networking and to wireless to my alma  mater Berkeley and for your inspiration  and your friendship and then this last  quote happiest person is the person who  thinks the most interesting thoughts and  we think about let's think about the  hilbert space right he's like let's look  at the simplest thing and do you see  what do you see is the role of this  let's look at the simple thing yeah you  know it's a very wonderfully insightful  comment in the following sense and let  me go back to this picture because I  think in some ways there is an analogy  here where this is kind of a very  general network and then you go to this  which is very simple right I mean it's  just you've got a bunch of antennas out  there and it removes the auspices of any  particular set of constraints like in a  hilbert space where you've got you know  it's it's a generalization of Euclidean  space so and and the problem with this  is that by removing levels of or making  the problem simpler here because now I  have no Wi-Fi or cellular I just have a  bunch of antennas it makes the problem  harder right because without those  constraints now we have to think about  certainly this optimization becomes a  lot harder and and so in some ways the  reason we as engineers design systems in  layers or as particular sets of  protocols is because it reduces the  design space down to something that we  can get our hands around when we do that  we throw out optimality it's always  better you know if you have something  more general so I think to go back to  the spirit of your question what I see  for the next wave of wireless and  networking research in general is and we  see this happening with software-defined  networks and an open flow and the work  that barefoot networks is doing is can  we throw off these constraints  that we've put on ourselves as engineers  because now we've had decades of  evolution of technology and  understanding about optimization and go  back to these problems and say let's  rethink them with all that knowledge and  maybe that will lead to a better  solution or maybe it will just make us  very frustrated because we won't come up  with the answers but I think it's a very  worthwhile endeavor  [Applause]
 

